@startuml ssh-key-auth
!include _style.iuml

title How the runner proves who it is — SSH public-key authentication

participant "**Client**\n(Actions runner)" as client #E7E0F5
participant "**Server**\nVPS <font:monospaced>sshd</font> :22" as server #DCE9F7

note across
  **This is the part that trips everyone up: there are two key pairs, pointing opposite ways.**

  **Host key**  — lives on the VPS. The *server* uses it to prove to the client
  "I really am this machine". The client remembers it in ""~/.ssh/known_hosts"".

  **User key**  — the pair you made with ""ssh-keygen"". The *client* uses it to prove
  to the server "I am allowed in as this user".
end note

== One-time setup (done by hand, once) ==

note over client, server
  ""ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "github-actions-edge-proxy"""

  produces two halves of one pair:
end note

client -> client : **private half** (""id_ed25519"")\npasted into the GitHub secret **VPS_SSH_KEY**
server -> server : **public half** (""id_ed25519.pub"")\nappended to ""~/.ssh/authorized_keys"" for VPS_USER

note over client, server #FFF8E1
  The public half is not a secret — it is safe in a repo, a chat message, a paste bin.
  The private half is the whole of the credential. Anything holding it can log in as
  that user. It is stored **encrypted** in GitHub Secrets, decrypted only into a
  running job's memory, and masked if it is ever echoed to a log.
end note

== Every connection, from scratch ==

autonumber "<b>[0]"

client -> server : TCP connect :22
server --> client : ""SSH-2.0-OpenSSH_9.6"" (version banner)
client --> server : ""SSH-2.0-Go"" (the action's client)

client <-> server : negotiate algorithms, then **key exchange**\n(ECDH — both sides derive the same session key,\nwithout that key ever crossing the wire)

server --> client : **host key** + a signature over the exchange hash
client -> client : is this host key in ""known_hosts""?
note right of client
  On a normal laptop this is the
  "authenticity of host ... can't be established" prompt.

  ""appleboy/ssh-action"" skips this check unless you pass
  ""fingerprint:"". Not verifying it means a machine that
  could intercept traffic to your VPS could impersonate it
  and collect the commands — it cannot, however, obtain
  your private key (see step 8).
end note

== everything below this line is encrypted ==

client -> server : ""userauth"" request: user = VPS_USER, method = **publickey**\n+ the **public** half, as a question: "would this be accepted?"
activate server
server -> server : look up ""~/.ssh/authorized_keys"" for that user\n→ is this exact public key listed?
server --> client : yes, that key is authorised — now prove you hold its private half
deactivate server

client -> client : build a challenge buffer:\n**session ID** + user + service + the public key\nthen **sign it with the private key**
note right of client
  The private key **never leaves the client** — not on the
  first connection, not ever. Only a signature crosses the
  wire, and because the buffer includes the session ID
  (unique per connection), that signature cannot be
  replayed against any other session.

  This is why SSH keys beat passwords: a password is sent to
  the server, so a hostile server learns it. A key signature
  proves possession without revealing the thing possessed.
end note
client -> server : signature

activate server
server -> server : verify the signature using the **public** key\nalready sitting in ""authorized_keys""
server --> client : ""SSH_MSG_USERAUTH_SUCCESS""
deactivate server

client -> server : open a session channel; run the deploy script
server --> client : stdout / stderr, then the **exit status**\n(non-zero here is what turns the job red)

@enduml